{
  "concept": "Two angles of depression measured from one horizontal reference at the airplane create two right triangles that share a single vertical altitude, and that shared side is the link that lets you solve for the plane's height and slant distances.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "airplane icon at upper left, nose pointing left",
    "teal dashed horizontal reference line extending right from the airplane (the horizontal at flight altitude)",
    "solid black horizontal highway line across the bottom",
    "point A on the highway (toward middle) with label 'A'",
    "point B on the highway (far right) with label 'B'",
    "gray dashed sight line from airplane to A (steeper)",
    "gray dashed sight line from airplane to B (shallower)",
    "angle arc at the airplane between the horizontal and the B sight line, labeled with the dynamic shallow depression angle (≈25°)",
    "angle arc at the airplane between the horizontal and the A sight line, labeled with the dynamic steep depression angle (≈49°)",
    "vertical altitude segment dropped from the airplane to the highway (revealed in the right-triangle phase) with a right-angle square marker at its foot",
    "horizontal distance tick/segment along the highway from the altitude foot to A and from A to B (revealed in the right-triangle phase)",
    "slant-distance and altitude value labels (revealed in the final phase)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "altitudeSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Airplane altitude",
      "range": [50, 300, 5],
      "default": 115,
      "teaches": "Raising or lowering the plane changes both depression angles together because tan(angle) = altitude / horizontal distance, so the altitude is the common vertical leg in both right triangles"
    },
    {
      "id": "abDistanceSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Distance A–B",
      "range": [50, 300, 5],
      "default": 150,
      "teaches": "Sliding B along the road changes only the shallow angle to B while the altitude and the steep angle to A stay put, making the shared-altitude constraint that couples the two triangles visible"
    },
    {
      "id": "solvePhase",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Solve",
      "states": ["Reference line", "Right triangles", "Reveal distances"],
      "default": "Reference line",
      "teaches": "Stepping through the construction shows how the horizontal reference defines the depression angles, how a dropped perpendicular turns the scene into two right triangles sharing one altitude, and how the unknown slant distances and height then follow from the tangent and sine ratios"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Set the horizontal reference",
      "narration": "The teal dashed line is the horizontal at flight altitude; each gray sight line opens downward from it, giving the 25° depression angle to B and the 49° depression angle to A from a single shared vertex.",
      "state": { "solvePhase": "Reference line", "altitudeSlider": 115, "abDistanceSlider": 150 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Form the two right triangles",
      "narration": "Dropping a perpendicular from the plane to the road splits the scene into two right triangles that share the same vertical altitude leg — the steep triangle to A and the shallow triangle to B.",
      "state": { "solvePhase": "Right triangles", "altitudeSlider": 115, "abDistanceSlider": 150 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reveal the unknown distances",
      "narration": "With the shared altitude linking the triangles, the tangent ratios give the horizontal distances and the sine ratios give the slant distances; drag the altitude or the A–B spacing to watch every labeled length and angle update consistently.",
      "state": { "solvePhase": "Reveal distances", "altitudeSlider": 115, "abDistanceSlider": 150 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Both angles of depression hang off one horizontal at the plane, so the two sight-line triangles share a single vertical altitude — that common side is the bridge that turns two angles and one road spacing into a solvable system for height and slant range.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Place the airplane icon at the upper left with its nose pointing left; run a teal dashed horizontal line to the right from it as the altitude reference. Across the bottom draw a solid black highway line with point A near the middle and point B at the far right, each labeled. From the airplane draw two gray dashed sight lines, the steeper one to A and the shallower one to B. At the airplane vertex draw two concentric angle arcs opening downward from the horizontal, the inner labeled with the live shallow angle (≈25°) and the outer with the live steep angle (≈49°); these numbers must recompute as sliders move. In the 'Right triangles' phase add a vertical altitude segment from the plane straight down to the highway with a small right-angle square at the foot and horizontal distance ticks to A and to B. In the 'Reveal distances' phase annotate the altitude value and the two slant distances. Keep the airplane, road, and reference line fixed in position while the sight lines, arcs, and revealed segments respond to the sliders."
}

